    a: "Both are ensemble methods that combine many models. Bagging trains models in parallel on random samples of the data and averages them, which mainly reduces variance; random forest is an example. Boosting trains models one after another, each focusing on the previous mistakes, which mainly reduces bias; gradient boosting and AdaBoost are examples.",
    code: "from sklearn.datasets import load_breast_cancer\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\nfrom sklearn.ensemble import BaggingClassifier, AdaBoostClassifier\n\nX, y = load_breast_cancer(return_X_y=True)\n\nmodels = {\n    \"single tree\": DecisionTreeClassifier(random_state=0),\n    \"bagging\": BaggingClassifier(n_estimators=50, random_state=0),\n    \"boosting\": AdaBoostClassifier(n_estimators=50, random_state=0),\n}\nfor name, m in models.items():\n    print(f\"{name:12s} CV accuracy={cross_val_score(m, X, y, cv=5).mean():.3f}\")",
