    q: "What is data leakage and how do pipelines prevent it?",
    level: 'Intermediate',
    a: "Data leakage is when information from the test set or from the future sneaks into training, so scores look great but the model fails in production. A common cause is scaling or imputing on the whole dataset before splitting. An sklearn Pipeline bundles preprocessing and the model, so during cross-validation each step is fit only on the training folds.",
    code: "from sklearn.datasets import load_breast_cancer\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\n\nX, y = load_breast_cancer(return_X_y=True)\n\npipe = Pipeline([\n    (\"impute\", SimpleImputer(strategy=\"median\")),\n    (\"scale\", StandardScaler()),\n    (\"model\", LogisticRegression()),\n])\nscores = cross_val_score(pipe, X, y, cv=5)\nprint(\"Steps:\", [name for name, _ in pipe.steps])\nprint(\"CV accuracy (no leakage):\", round(scores.mean(), 3))",
