    q: "What are hallucinations and how do you reduce them?",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "A hallucination is when an LLM confidently produces something false, like a fake fact or a made-up citation, because it predicts likely text rather than checking truth. I reduce it with RAG to ground answers in real documents, instructions to answer only from the context and say 'I don't know', lower temperature, asking for citations, and validating outputs. A simple check is whether the answer is actually supported by the retrieved context.",
    code: "def grounded(answer, context):\n    # crude check: share of answer words that appear in the context\n    words = [w.strip(\".,\").lower() for w in answer.split()]\n    ctx = context.lower()\n    supported = sum(w in ctx for w in words)\n    return round(supported / len(words), 2)\n\ncontext = \"The Eiffel Tower is in Paris and was completed in 1889.\"\nanswers = [\"The Eiffel Tower was completed in 1889.\",\n           \"The Eiffel Tower was built by Romans in 450.\"]\nfor a in answers:\n    score = grounded(a, context)\n    print(f\"{score:.2f} {'OK' if score > 0.8 else 'POSSIBLE HALLUCINATION'}: {a}\")",
    output: "1.00 OK: The Eiffel Tower was completed in 1889.\n0.56 POSSIBLE HALLUCINATION: The Eiffel Tower was built by Romans in 450.",
