    q: "What is a vector database?",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "A vector database stores embeddings and quickly finds the vectors most similar to a query vector. Examples are Pinecone, Weaviate, Milvus, Chroma, and pgvector for Postgres. They use approximate nearest neighbor indexes like HNSW so search stays fast over millions of vectors, and they also store metadata for filtering. Here sklearn's NearestNeighbors acts as a tiny in-memory version.",
    code: "import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\nids = [\"doc_cats\", \"doc_dogs\", \"doc_cars\", \"doc_bikes\"]\nvectors = np.array([[0.9, 0.1, 0.0], [0.8, 0.3, 0.1],\n                    [0.0, 0.2, 0.9], [0.1, 0.3, 0.8]])\n\nindex = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=2, metric=\"cosine\").fit(vectors)\n\nquery = np.array([[0.85, 0.2, 0.05]])        # \"pets\" query embedding\ndist, idx = index.kneighbors(query)\nfor d, i in zip(dist[0], idx[0]):\n    print(f\"{ids[i]:10s} similarity={1 - d:.3f}\")",
