    a: "I build a small test set of real questions with expected answers, then measure things like correctness, faithfulness to the context, relevance and format. I use automatic checks (exact match, keyword or JSON validity), LLM-as-a-judge for open-ended answers, and human review for a sample. I run this eval on every prompt or model change, like unit tests, and track latency and cost too.",
    code: "tests = [\n    {\"q\": \"Capital of France?\", \"expected\": \"paris\",\n     \"got\": \"The capital is Paris.\"},\n    {\"q\": \"2 + 2?\", \"expected\": \"4\", \"got\": \"It is 4.\"},\n    {\"q\": \"Largest planet?\", \"expected\": \"jupiter\",\n     \"got\": \"Saturn is the largest.\"},\n]\n\npassed = 0\nfor t in tests:\n    ok = t[\"expected\"] in t[\"got\"].lower()\n    passed += ok\n    print(f\"{'PASS' if ok else 'FAIL'}  {t['q']}\")\nprint(f\"Score: {passed}/{len(tests)} = {passed / len(tests):.0%}\")",
