    q: "What is MLOps and how do you monitor models for drift?",
    level: 'Advanced',
    a: "MLOps applies DevOps practices to ML: version data and models, automate training and testing, deploy with CI/CD, and monitor in production. Data drift means the input data in production starts looking different from the training data, and concept drift means the relationship between input and target changes. I monitor feature distributions with tests like KS or PSI, track live accuracy when labels arrive, and retrain when drift is detected.",
    code: "import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import ks_2samp\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\ntrain_age = rng.normal(35, 5, 1000)          # data the model was trained on\nweek1_age = rng.normal(35, 5, 1000)          # production: same distribution\nweek9_age = rng.normal(45, 5, 1000)          # production: users got older\n\nfor name, live in [(\"week 1\", week1_age), (\"week 9\", week9_age)]:\n    stat, p = ks_2samp(train_age, live)\n    status = \"DRIFT - retrain\" if p < 0.05 else \"OK\"\n    print(f\"{name}: mean={live.mean():.1f} KS={stat:.3f} -> {status}\")",
